in this video I want to explore the  question of what impact does the  environment have on human migration and  we have a couple specific examples here  and before we dig into those I want to  make a few broader points about the  environment as a historical factor and  the first of those is simply that the  environment does have impacts on what  people are able to do on how and where  they're able to move we might be looking  at is the climate suitable for  practicing agriculture food production  is such an important part of a human  movement in human survival in new  environments we might look at  transportation in the Pacific Ocean for  example getting from island to island  requires that you have technology that  allows you to travel over the ocean from  long distances the second point to  consider more broadly is that human  beings when they move to new areas tend  to have impacts on the environment so if  they're bringing new plants or new  animals to a place where they haven't  previously existed that's going to have  some impacts on the new place and  finally this isn't to say that the  environment is wholly explanatory of why  history played out the way it did but we  also don't want to dismiss the  environment as a potential explanatory  factor so with those bigger points in  mind let's start by looking at a  specific example in Africa known as the  Bantu migration or more often the Bantu  expansion into sub-saharan Africa and  when I say sub-saharan Africa that's  just the region of Africa that is south  of the Sahara Desert and I'll shade that  in here so that you can see where I'm  talking about and next what I have is an  overlay in yellow that shows the areas  of Africa where a Bantu language is the  the predominant language spoken so the  question that raises is why does so much  of sub-saharan Africa speak a Bantu  language there there aren't any written  records of Bantu speakers and and how  they moved and what they were thinking  when they did move but there is evidence  in terms of how the languages relate to  one another and there's also  archaeological evidence so one of the  helpful things about studying the bed to  expansion is that from about 500 400 BCE  on they were using iron tools they had  iron making technology and those tools  along with Potter  that's distinctive too to bantu-speaking  cultures can help archaeologists trace  how Bantu speakers spread throughout  Africa over time so linguists who have  studied how these languages relate to  one another and archaeologists who have  looked at evidence of how Bantu speakers  migrated and moved throughout Africa  are fairly confident that they  originated somewhere here in West Africa  and from there spread to the south as  well as to the east over to what's known  as the Great Lakes region of Africa and  then from there farther south again so  that tells us how people moved through  Africa what pattern did they follow the  next question we want to try and figure  out the answer to is why did they  migrate to the places that they did well  one thing that we know about  bantu-speaking peoples is that they  tended to have fairly consistent and  common agricultural practices and among  the crops that they were growing were  millet and sorghum once they got to the  Great Lakes region and started moving  south you also see them adopting cattle  herding pictures of the cattle that they  would have probably had here and what  these crops in these cattle have in  common all of them tend to thrive in  what's known as a tropical savanna or a  semi tropical savanna climate and  sub-saharan Africa with the exception of  this rainforest region here and this  high desert down in the South is largely  tropical or semi tropical savanna and  when we put the overlay back up of where  Bantu speakers are the majority we can  see that sort of corresponds with those  climate variations you see them populate  really all of the areas that are  favorable to to their agricultural  practices and that should make sense if  we think about migration as a  risk/reward calculation you need to make  sure that any place that you're  migrating to is going to be able to  provide you with food so to bring that  back to our big theme how does  environment impact migration we really  see in the Bantu expansion the role of  climate affecting what kind of  agriculture as possible and that  determines what areas look attractive  for migration so the next region we want  to look at the next migration we want to  study is that of people moving  throughout the Pacific island  you can see the path this migration  probably took here and when we talk  about expansion in the Pacific we're  looking at something somewhat similar to  the Bantu expansion in Africa and that  again we don't have any written records  from the people who moved here so again  we're relying on linguistic or language  based evidence as well as archaeological  evidence and this evidence helps us sort  of divide up the Pacific into three  distinct regions so we have what's  called Melanesia in blue and Micronesia  in red and Polynesia in purple and again  these are all people who were originally  speaking some form of Austronesian  language very similar culturally but  they can look at how there have been  small changes in cultural artifacts  changes in languages and even in the  genetics of the people and the animals  in different regions of the Pacific to  kind of get a sense of of where they  were at what time and that gives us this  this probable route of migration that we  saw so thinking again about environment  impacting this migration into the  Pacific Ocean  there are a couple of important factors  the first and obvious one is that  getting across large stretches of ocean  is going to pose a difficulty it's an  obstacle and the way that people  overcame this obstacle in the Pacific  was they came up with better sailing  technology and I can pull up here a  picture of a recreation of a catamaran  this technology was really important for  travelling long distances in the ocean  because to get out in the open ocean you  need a sturdy a durable ship that's  going to hold up to wind and waves so  even though this is a modern recreation  it gives us a good sense of what the  technology was like and you can see how  that that would be a more durable craft  than say canoe by itself so this allows  people to travel between islands the  other issue  environmentally speaking that poses an  obstacle to expansion in the Pacific is  that a lot of these islands are really  small you're probably noticing that some  of them are quite difficult to see on  the satellite image and they don't have  a lot of arable or farmable land on them  and so they could cancel  or large populations and this is  probably one reason why there is  continued expansion in the Pacific over  a long period of time is because the  islands themselves can't support very  large populations if the population  grows to a certain point some people  have to leave so to deal with this fact  that the islands didn't provide a lot of  natural food people would bring their  own food supplies with them so they  bring things like sweet potatoes and  yams and taro root and they would bring  animals as well so chickens and you have  a picture here this is what's called a  Southeast Asian red jungle fowl and it's  the precursor to to domestic chickens so  it can give you a sense of what some of  these chickens probably looked like they  brought pigs they also had a small breed  of dog the dog breed is extinct now but  we have some pictures that were drawn by  some of the early European explorers of  the Pacific so people would bring these  items with them they'd bring these  animals with them onto these new islands  they would grow the crops they would  raise the animals and use them as a food  source and to go back to our original  list of a big takeaways this gets into  this idea of environmental impact of  bringing these new plants and animals so  what happens is that people bring these  plants they bring these animals that are  not native to these islands on to these  islands and use them they farm them they  raise them for food and in most cases  people are able to sort of strike a  balance of maintaining a food supply  with these crops and animals that  they've brought and not destroying the  island environment because obviously  that would have consequences for their  ability to continue to live there  hopefully it's clear at this point that  environment definitely played a role in  these migrations in these expansions  certainly this isn't only about  environmental factors but in both cases  it would be very difficult to explain  what happened if you didn't include  these environmental factors
 

